    {
      "id": "graph-native-chemistry-instructions",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Reading This Idea",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": -220,
        "y": 700
      },
      "width": 760,
      "height": 300,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        },
        {
          "id": "top",
          "label": "top",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "value",
          "angle": 270
        },
        {
          "id": "right",
          "label": "right",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "value",
          "angle": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "bottom",
          "label": "bottom",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "value",
          "angle": 90
        },
        {
          "id": "left",
          "label": "left",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "value",
          "angle": 180
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "graph-native-chemistry"
        },
        "markdown": "## Read this as a product idea, not a chemistry lesson\n\nThe core proposition is that Twilite can model a **canonical library of element resources**, then reuse those resources in multiple periodic-table views and in richer chemistry graphs where **bond semantics live on edges**.\n\nThe next step should be a deliberately small prototype: a handful of element graphs, two alternative table views, and molecule specimens that prove single, double, and triple bond rendering."
      }
    },
